# surface — what a hundred years outdoors does to the stone.
#
# This fragment holds almost no geometry. What it holds is WEATHER: the statements that make a
# stone building read as a stone building that has stood in the rain rather than as a drawing of
# one delivered this morning. Sand and dust at the foot of the steps and in the hollows of the
# mouldings; the sun bleaching the treads while the risers stay clean; the sharp arrises of a
# cornice worn round while the hollow behind them keeps its edge; moss and lichen on the north
# side, where the wall never dries.
#
# THE FILE IS IN TWO HALVES AND THEY ARE NOT THE SAME KIND OF THING. Down to the two `weather`
# statements is the ENGINE's weathering — two lines that put `Weather::Desert`, `Weather::
# Overgrown`, `Op::Curvature`, `Op::Occlusion`, `Op::Facing` and `WeatherRequest::scope` under
# load, and cost the building six hundred times its sample time to do it. Everything after them is
# the FINISHING COAT: seventeen hand-written rules that weather the whole ninety-four metres of
# elevation for a twentieth of that, because a rule keyed on a pattern and placed on a shape has a
# bounding box and reads nothing but the point it is asked about. Read the header of that section
# before touching either half; they solve the same problem and they are not interchangeable.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THIS FILE PUTS UNDER LOAD: the weathering subsystem, and the three field operations that
# exist only to serve it.
#
#   Weather::Desert       curvature-driven scour, and two coats keyed on facing and on height
#   Weather::Overgrown    occlusion-driven growth, and two coats keyed on cavity and facing
#   Op::Curvature         seven evaluations of the whole building per point. Desert's only input.
#   Op::Occlusion         fourteen. Overgrown's only input.
#   Op::Facing            six, through normal_at. All four coats read it.
#   WeatherRequest::scope the `on=` half — the reason the last facility shipped with no weathering
#                         at all, and the only part of this that is new
#
# Nothing else in the building weathers except one hairline coat on the site's boundary plinth
# (`site.clip`, `weather cracks 0.10 on=site_weather_zone`), and cracks reads none of the three
# operations above. Delete the two `weather` statements at the bottom of this file and
# `Op::Curvature`, `Op::Occlusion` and `Op::Facing` are dead code again, along with
# `WeatherRequest::scope`, and four of the five entries in `enum class Weather` have never been
# run on anything. That is why this file exists and it is the whole of what it does.
# DO NOT DELETE THE WEATHER STATEMENTS BECAUSE THE FILE LOOKS EMPTY.
#
# It is also the only place in the building where a material is invented rather than declared:
# `apply_weather` calls `make_material` for `sand`, `bleached`, `moss` and `lichen`, none of which
# is in `_contract.clip`. So this fragment adds four materials to the palette that nothing else
# uses — four more rows in the voxel type table and four more BSDFs in a frame.
#
# READ clips/facility/requests/surface.md BEFORE SWITCHING THIS ON, BECAUSE IT IS NOT FREE.
#
# Every coat these two kinds add is unsettleable, so it is asked once per solid voxel of the WHOLE
# clip, and each ask is twenty evaluations of the whole building's distance field. Measured: one
# line costs a small clip 12x its sample time and the two together 114x; on real facility geometry
# — site, podium, steps and walls at metre 8 — the pair costs 362 ms -> 237,303 ms, which is 656x.
# The complete building with these two lines in it did not finish at metre 5 in forty-five minutes.
#
# That is a property of the two KINDS and not of the scope. `on=` is free, and it is the half of
# this that works exactly as advertised: measured against a control with the same weather lines
# and their zones moved into empty air, not one voxel of lawn, water or bronze changed and all
# 93,950 weathered voxels came out of stone. The request file has the numbers, and the two engine
# changes that would make the rest of it affordable.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# THE MODULE. Every box below is a multiple or a simple fraction of M = 0.45, with three named
# exceptions, all of them heights taken from the elevation rather than invented: 2.40 is the
# walls' first rustication joint (podium_top plus one 0.60 course), 11.475 is 25.5 M and 5.40 and
# 5.85 are 12 M and 13 M — see each zone.
#
# WHERE THE ZONES ARE AND WHY. The building faces south, at negative z. So:
#
#   the south and the top   sun, wind and dust. Desert.
#   the north              shade and damp. Overgrown.
#
# A building weathers by which way it faces, so the fragment that weathers it is organised the
# same way. And every boundary of every zone is put on an arris the elevation already has — a
# bed mould's springing, a rustication joint, the top of a string course — because the mask has a
# soft edge about 0.05 m wide and an edge that lands in the middle of a flat face reads as a line
# somebody drew. `site.clip` learned this first and its note is worth reading.

include "_contract.clip"

# ================================================================================================
# THE PART — two pieces of weathering that are matter rather than colour
# ================================================================================================
#
# THE SETTING-OUT STONE IS GONE, AND IT WAS NEVER IN THE BUILDING. For this fragment's whole life
# `part_surface` was one buried half-module cube — `box -0.1125 0.5625 -2.3625 0.1125 0.7875
# -2.1375` — put in the middle of the podium's mass on the centre line because the manifest's
# unions want a `part_<name>` from every fragment and this file's output was two statements rather
# than a shape. It was painted `paint granite where=surface_datum below=0.02`.
#
# It is inside `crypt_room`, which is `box -11.70 -0.4625 -5.70  11.70 1.6875 5.70`, and
# `void_crypt` is subtracted from `built` in the manifest. So the cube was excavated by the crypt
# on the first build after crypt.clip was written, the paint rule had no voxel to land on, and both
# have been dead ever since. Nothing said so: the fragment reported one component, the manifest
# counted twenty-seven parts and twelve voids and balanced, and `clipcheck` could not tell anybody
# because its paint diagnostic counted evaluations rather than matches and its counter was never
# switched on. It was found from the assembly end, by auditing what each `difference` removes.
#
# There is no replacement stone because there no longer needs to be one: this fragment now has real
# geometry, built in section 9 — a cove of soil in the angle where the north wall stands on the
# terrace, and a row of moss cushions on the north string course. `part_surface` is bound to those
# two at the bottom of the file, where the noise that displaces them is already defined. Both stand
# outside every void in the building and both are checked against the ones that come nearest.
#
# The claim this file used to make, that it changes no geometry, is no longer true.

# This fragment hollows nothing. Said out loud, as the template asks, so the manifest never has to
# wonder whether a fragment carved something:
# there is no void_surface.

# ================================================================================================
# THE ZONES — boxes, and the reason they are boxes
# ================================================================================================
#
# `weather ... on=<shape>` evaluates the named shape once per voxel of the whole clip, because the
# scope compiles into every coat's test and `metric_slack(Op::Smoothstep)` is infinite, so the
# sampler can never settle it for a block. The site fragment measured 1,453 ms for a four-box
# scope against 574,333 ms for a scope naming its own stonework — the same coat over the same
# stone, two hundred and eleven times the cost.
#
# So every zone here is a box, or a difference of two boxes, and never `on=part_walls`.
#
# They are also written so that nothing can get them wrong by being generous. A weathering coat is
# appended AFTER every fragment's paint and would sand a bronze urn or bleach a hedge as readily
# as it dusts a step, so each zone is checked against its neighbours and the check is written down.

# --- the sunny side: the great steps and the podium face they climb to -------------------------
#
# x to +-9.90 (22 M): the flight is +-9.45 and this is a module beyond its cheek walls.
# z from -16.50 to -12.60 (28 M): past the bottom nosing, to a module INSIDE the podium's south
# face at -12.825, so the far edge of the mask is buried in stone and cannot show.
# y from -0.45 (-M) to 1.80 (podium_top): the terrace is the ceiling, which is a real arris.
#
# The floor is below the ground on purpose, so that the gravel forecourt takes the drift at the
# foot of the flight, which is where sand actually ends up. The zone is gravel and not lawn:
# site.clip paints the whole southern approach gravel from the boundary out to z = -13.05. The two
# reflecting basins start at x = 11.70 and the site's bronze urns stand at x = +-10.575, both
# well outside +-9.90.
#
# THE NEAR EDGE MOVED WITH THE STAIR AND THIS ZONE HAD NOT. steps.clip's flight used to run
# z -15.70 to -12.41 and now runs -16.25 to -12.96, because the top nosing had to land on the
# podium's south face at -13.05 rather than 0.55 m north of it. At -15.75 this zone stopped half a
# metre short of the bottom four risers, so the one part of the flight that actually collects drift
# — the foot of it, on the gravel — was outside the desert scope. -16.50 is the clip's own bounds
# and not a module multiple, which is why it is written here rather than derived: there is no
# module line left between the bottom nosing at -16.25 and the edge of the sampled box.
let surface_sun_steps = box -9.90 -0.45 -16.50   9.90 1.80 -12.60

# --- the sunny side: the wash of the main cornice ----------------------------------------------
#
# The corona and the crowning cyma at the top of the entablature — the one band of the elevation
# that the rain reaches and the sun stands on all day, which is what a mason calls the wash.
#
# A RING and not a box: 0.675 m (1.5 M) wide round the outside of the building, y from 11.475
# (25.5 M, just under the corona's bottom arris at 11.52) to 12.15 (27 M, air).
#
# The ring is what keeps this off everything it must not touch, and every number in it is a
# clearance:
#   the lead roof deck    x +-15.85, z +-7.35   -> the inner box at +-16.20 and 7.65 excludes it
#   the parapet and dies  out to about x 16.00  -> same
#   the parapet's urns    y 12.25 and above     -> under the 12.15 ceiling
#   the pediment corners  y 11.85 to 12.35 at z -12.1  -> inside the inner box in z, excluded
#   the entablature's bronze swags and paterae, on the frieze at 10.55 to 11.15 -> below 11.475
let surface_wash_out = box -16.875 11.475 -13.950   16.875 12.150  8.550
let surface_wash_in  = box -16.200 11.250 -13.050   16.200 12.600  7.650
let surface_wash     = difference { surface_wash_out surface_wash_in }

let surface_sun = union { surface_sun_steps surface_wash }

# --- the damp side: the foot of the north wall -------------------------------------------------
#
# North is +z and the north wall is the one that never dries. This box takes the podium's north
# bed mould and corona and the first rusticated course of the wall above it.
#
# y from 1.35 (3 M) — the springing of the podium's bed mould, a real edge — to 2.40, which is the
# walls' first horizontal rustication joint. 2.40 is not a module multiple and it is not invented
# either: it is podium_top plus one 0.60 course, and it is the one line the elevation already has
# at that height. The soft edge of the mask lands in a 0.09 m channel and disappears. It also
# clears the ground-storey window sills, which start at 2.52, so no glass and no glazing bar is
# in this zone.
#
# z from 6.75 (15 M) to 8.55 (19 M). The wall's outer face is at 7.50 and its INNER face at 6.60,
# so the box lies entirely within the thickness of the wall and outward of it: nothing inside the
# building is in it. That matters — moss belongs on a wall that can see the sky, and the rotunda
# is two metres further in. The site's north hedge is below y = 0.27 and nowhere near.
let surface_damp_foot = box -16.875 1.35 6.75   16.875 2.40 8.55

# --- the damp side: the north string course ----------------------------------------------------
#
# The one projecting ledge on the north elevation, and the best thing in the building for this: a
# bold corona with a flat soffit and an ovolo weathering above it, standing out to z = 7.68. An
# up-facing ledge on a north wall is where growth starts and the hollow under its soffit is where
# it stays.
#
# y 5.40 to 5.85 is 12 M to 13 M AND is exactly the corona's bottom arris and the ovolo's top, so
# both edges of the mask land on edges that are carved. The piano nobile's sills are at 6.12 and
# the ground storey's arched keystones top out at 5.40, so the zone touches stone and nothing else.
let surface_damp_ledge = box -16.875 5.40 7.20   16.875 5.85 8.10

let surface_damp = union { surface_damp_foot surface_damp_ledge }

# ================================================================================================
# THE WEATHER
# ================================================================================================
#
# DESERT FIRST AND OVERGROWN SECOND, AND THAT IS NOT A STYLISTIC ORDERING. `apply_weather` re-reads
# `script.solid` at the top of every request and every kind ends by displacing it, so the second
# request occludes a field the first has already deepened. Written this way round the pair costs
# 47.0 s on the bench clip; written the other way round, for identical output, it costs 84.8 s.
# 1.8x, for swapping two lines. Do not swap them.
#
# LOW AMOUNTS. This is a public building that is looked after, not a ruin.
#
#   desert 0.18     The scour displaces at most 0.05 x scale x amount = 9 mm, and only where the
#                   curvature says arris — about a third of a voxel at metre 32, which softens a
#                   nosing without eating it. `level = -0.45` is the datum the sand drifts up from,
#                   set a module BELOW the lawn so the drift dies out about a metre up the flight
#                   instead of covering it: the height term is one at the level and zero 1.5 x
#                   scale above it. The bleach coat's threshold is 0.55 - 0.35a = 0.487 against a
#                   test of 0.6 x facing + 0.4 x grain, so it lands on up-facing surfaces where
#                   the grain is over about -0.28 and NEVER on a vertical one, where the test
#                   cannot reach 0.4 however long it stands there. Treads bleach, risers stay
#                   clean. That is what rain does and it is the one part of this that is free.
#
#   overgrown 0.18  Swells the surface by at most 0.045 x scale x amount = 7 mm where growth takes
#                   hold. Lichen's threshold is 0.55 - 0.35a = 0.487 and moss's is 0.85 - 0.55a =
#                   0.751, against 0.55 x cavity + 0.35 x facing + 0.4 x clumps. A flat exposed
#                   wall face has a cavity of about 0.5 and a facing of zero, so it reaches
#                   0.275 + clumps: patchy lichen, and no moss at all, ever, at any amount below
#                   about 0.9. A re-entrant corner with an up-facing ledge in it reaches 0.76
#                   before the clumps are counted, so the moss goes exactly where the ledge meets
#                   the wall and the soffit meets the corona. That is the whole argument for
#                   driving this off occlusion instead of off a texture, and on the bench clip it
#                   is the difference between a green band along a cornice and green paint.
#
# The four materials these lines invent — sand 198,176,132; bleached 208,202,188; moss 74,108,54;
# lichen 138,148,108 — are made by `make_material` at the colours written into clip_script.cpp,
# because _contract.clip does not declare them and this fragment deliberately does not either.
# Four names belonging to the whole building should not be claimed by one fragment; the request
# file asks for them to be added to the contract instead.

weather desert    0.18 scale=1.00 level=-0.45 seed=83 on=surface_sun
weather overgrown 0.18 scale=0.90 seed=89 on=surface_damp

# ================================================================================================
# THE FINISHING COAT — the weathering that is written by hand, and why it is not `weather`
# ================================================================================================
#
# Everything above this line is the ENGINE's weathering, and the request file next door says what
# it costs: `Weather::Desert` and `Weather::Overgrown` are keyed on `Op::Occlusion`, `Op::Facing`
# and `Op::Curvature`, all three of which read `script.solid` — the whole assembled building —
# several times over per voxel, and neither kind can be settled for a block. Two lines cost the
# cut-down facility 362 ms -> 237,303 ms. They stay, because they are the only thing in this
# repository that exercises those three field operations and the `on=` scope, and deleting them
# would make four fifths of `enum class Weather` dead code again.
#
# But they are two lines, they reach two places, and the building has ninety-four metres of
# elevation. So everything below is the same job done with the tool that scales: a paint rule
# keyed on a PATTERN and placed on a SHAPE, which `sample.cpp` gives a bounding box and prunes,
# and which reads nothing but the point it is asked about. `walls.clip` invented the idiom for its
# ashlar joints and it is used here fifteen times over:
#
#     let dirty = max { <a zone shape>  add { constant <t>  negate { <a pattern> } } }
#     paint tuff where=dirty below=0.035
#
# `max` is intersection written arithmetically, so this is at most nought only where the point is
# BOTH inside the zone AND above the threshold; and `Field::bounds_of` gives a `max` the tighter of
# its children's boxes, so the whole rule carries the zone's box and costs nothing anywhere else.
# Nothing below evaluates the solid, so nothing below is charged for the building.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT SEPARATES A SCAN FROM A RENDER, WHICH IS THE WHOLE BRIEF FOR THIS SECTION
#
#   DIRT OBEYS GRAVITY AND WATER.   Every stain here runs DOWNWARD, because the noise it is cut
#                                   from is stretched eight times along y (`scale { fbm } y=8`),
#                                   and it starts under a drip line and dies out below it, because
#                                   the threshold is faded by a `smoothstep` of the y coordinate.
#                                   A soffit that rain cannot reach is PALER than the face beside
#                                   it, and the top of every horizontal is dirtier than its face.
#                                   A uniform grime layer is the giveaway and there is none here:
#                                   every rule is a zone times a pattern times a height.
#
#   MATERIALS AGE DIFFERENTLY.      Bronze goes green where water sits on it and stays brown-gold
#                                   where a hand touches it; limestone bleaches on the south front
#                                   and blackens on the north; the same wall is four materials
#                                   between the ground and the cornice.
#
#   BIOLOGY LIVES WHERE WATER SITS. Moss is in the bed joints of the NORTH rustication and on the
#                                   north string course and nowhere else. Lichen is on the tops
#                                   that stand in the open. Neither is sprayed over a face.
#
#   WEAR FOLLOWS PEOPLE.            The portico floor and the great door's threshold are walked
#                                   dark and smooth down the middle; the door leaves are polished
#                                   bright at 1.08 m above the floor, which is where the ring
#                                   handles are and where a hand goes.
#
#   AND SOME OF IT IS MATTER.       At 32 voxels to the metre a voxel is 31 mm, so a moss cushion
#                                   and a cove of soil in the angle of a wall are SHAPE and not
#                                   colour. Both are built below, in `part_surface`, and both are
#                                   displaced by a noise so that no two metres of them are alike.
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT DO, AND WHY — said here rather than left to be discovered:
#
#   NO CHIPPED ARRISES, NO DISHED TREADS, NO HOLLOWED THRESHOLD. All three are subtractions, and
#   this fragment cannot subtract. `clips/facility.clip` takes matter away in exactly one place —
#   `let hollowed = difference { built void_doors void_windows void_vestibule ... }` — and there
#   is no `void_surface` in that list. A fragment that wants to remove matter from the finished
#   building needs one line in the manifest and this file is not allowed to write it. Everything
#   this fragment adds is therefore additive, and the two matter pieces below are the two kinds of
#   weathering that genuinely ADD material rather than take it away. When somebody adds
#   `void_surface` to that difference, the chipping belongs here.
#
#   NOTHING TOUCHES THE WINDOWS, and the exclusion is arithmetic rather than a promise.
#   `windows.clip` says DO NOT WEATHER THIS PART: its glazing bars are 0.030 m across, one voxel,
#   and a displacement of 0.012 is 40% of one. Every joinery bar and every pane in this building
#   is RECESSED BEHIND THE WALL FACE — `windows_pn_barv` and `windows_ga_barv` sit at local
#   z = 0.165..0.2475 and the glass at 0.225..0.27, against a wall face at local z = 0 — so on the
#   south front they are at z = -7.335..-7.2525, on the north at 7.2525..7.4175, and on the ends at
#   |x| = 15.7525..15.835. EVERY PAINT zone below that touches a wall face stops 0.06 m behind that
#   face at the deepest (z = -7.45 south, 7.47 north, |x| = 15.94 on the ends), which is 0.09 m
#   clear of the nearest bar on every front. Two things go deeper and both are checked: the moss
#   rule is deliberately IN the north rustication channels at z 7.36..7.47, which is still 0.055
#   short of a bar and three and a half metres below the nearest one; and the soil cove, which is
#   matter rather than paint, laps to z 7.42 at y 1.72..2.02, against a lowest window sill in this
#   building of 2.385. The sills, jambs and cornices those windows are set in are stone and are
#   weathered like the rest of the elevation; the joinery and the glass are not reached at all.
#
#   NOTHING IS DECLARED. Every material below is one `_contract.clip` already holds. The contract
#   is the one place a material is declared and a weathering coat is a poor reason to break that,
#   so the palette is read rather than extended: `tuff` for grime, `slate` for the black runnels
#   and the walked path, `sandstone` for splash, `bark` for the soil line, `plaster` for a dry
#   soffit, `marble_hot` for sun-bleach, `verde` for the damp north, `hedge` for moss,
#   `travertine` for lichen, `copper` and `malachite` for verdigris, `ormolu` for polished bronze,
#   `terracotta` for an iron stain, `gravel` for wind-blown dust.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ================================================================================================
# THE GRAMMAR — five patterns, and every stain in the building is made of them
# ================================================================================================
#
# `scale` with unequal factors is what turns a blob into a run. The field evaluates the child at
# p/(1, 8, 1), so a feature 0.36 m across becomes 0.36 across and 2.9 m long — which is a stain
# that ran down a wall rather than a patch of noise. It costs the node its bounding box (an
# unequal scale under-reports distance and `field.cpp` refuses to bound it), and that costs
# nothing here because every one of these is used inside a `max` with a zone, and `max` takes the
# tighter box of the two.
let s_run    = scale { fbm size=0.36 octaves=4 seed=311 } x=1 y=8 z=1
let s_run_bd = scale { fbm size=0.90 octaves=3 seed=317 } x=1 y=6 z=1

# The mottle a piece of stone weathers in, and the grit that keeps every edge in this file ragged.
# Nothing below is thresholded on a smooth noise alone: a smooth threshold draws a smooth line and
# a smooth line is a drawing.
let s_patch  = fbm size=0.60 octaves=4 seed=331
let s_grit   = fbm size=0.08 octaves=3 seed=337
let s_moss_n = fbm size=0.28 octaves=3 seed=349

# The three working patterns. `blend { a b } t` is a*(1-t) + b*t, so each of these is the broad
# shape of a stain with a fifth of the fine grit mixed into it, and no threshold in this file is
# ever taken against a smooth noise on its own.
let s_streak = blend { s_run   s_grit } t=0.22
let s_bloom  = blend { s_patch s_grit } t=0.25
let s_wash   = blend { s_run_bd s_grit } t=0.18

# ================================================================================================
# THE SKINS — how far a rule is allowed to reach into a face
# ================================================================================================
#
# 0.06 outward and 0.05 inward of the face it names, and both numbers are chosen rather than
# comfortable. Outward, because `paint ... below` is a distance test at a voxel's CENTRE and a
# surface voxel's centre can sit a little outside the shape it belongs to. Inward, because a zone
# that reaches deep into a wall makes the sampler walk every buried voxel of it — a paint rule
# keyed on a pattern cannot be settled for a block, so the cost of one of these is the SOLID
# VOLUME OF ITS ZONE and nothing else. Every zone here is a skin over a face for that reason, and
# the total comes to about forty cubic metres of stone against the building's three thousand.
#
# So every face zone below is written to the same three numbers and they are worth reading once:
#
#   the south wall face  z = -7.50   ->  a zone from -7.66 to -7.45
#   the north wall face  z =  7.50   ->  a zone from  7.45 to  7.66
#   the end wall faces  |x| = 16.00  ->  a zone from 15.95 to 16.17
#
# The outer figure clears the quoins, which stand 0.135 proud (`walls_quoins`); the inner figure is
# the one that keeps every rule off the joinery, as the note at the top works out.
#
# The south front is taken in two pieces either side of the portico, |x| from 7.70 outward. Inside
# the portico the wall is under a 5 m soffit and has never been rained on, which is why the streak
# rules stop there and a dust rule takes over.

# ================================================================================================
# 1 — WATER: THE STRING COURSE IS THE BUILDING'S BIGGEST DRIP LINE
# ================================================================================================
#
# `walls.clip` puts a bold string course at y 5.31..5.85 standing 0.18 proud of the face all the
# way round (`walls_sc_band_n` reaches z = 7.680 against a wall at 7.500). Everything that falls on
# the piano nobile above it is thrown clear of the wall by that band and lands on the rusticated
# storey below — so the streaks start at 5.34, under its soffit, and are gone by about 3.4.
#
# The fade is what makes that true: `smoothstep { axis of=y } from=3.20 to=5.30` is one at the
# string course and nought two metres under it, and it MULTIPLIES the pattern, so the threshold is
# never reached at the bottom of the band however the noise falls. It is also the cheap factor and
# it is written first, because `Op::Multiply` stops at the first factor that is nought and the fbm
# after it is then never evaluated at all.
let s_str_s  = mirror { box  7.70 3.20 -7.66  16.12 5.34 -7.45 } axis=x
let s_str_n  = box   -16.12 3.20  7.45  16.12 5.34  7.66
let s_str_ew = mirror { box 15.95 3.20 -7.62  16.17 5.34  7.62 } axis=x
let s_str_zone = union { s_str_s s_str_n s_str_ew }

let s_str_fade = smoothstep { axis of=y } from=3.20 to=5.30
let s_str_wet  = multiply { s_str_fade s_streak }

# ================================================================================================
# 2 — SUN AND SHADE, BEFORE ANYTHING RUNS OVER THEM
# ================================================================================================
#
# The building faces south, so the south front stands in the sun all day and the north front never
# dries. Two coats, both broad, both under everything else, because an aspect is the ground a
# stain is seen against and not a mark on top of it.
#
# South: the corona and crowning cyma of the main entablature (y 11.52..11.90, out to z = -8.22),
# the ashlar of the wings above the string course, and the podium's south face. Bleached warm and
# bright — `marble_hot` is the contract's own sunlit stone.
let s_sun_cornice = mirror { box  7.70  9.95 -8.30  16.30 11.92 -7.86 } axis=x
let s_sun_ashlar  = mirror { box  7.70  5.90 -7.66  16.12  9.85 -7.45 } axis=x
# The podium band starts at 1.45 and not at the ground, and that is a clearance rather than a
# choice: `site_hedge_s_a` and `site_hedge_s_b` are clipped hedges standing to y = 1.35 against the
# podium's south face at x 10.35..11.25, and a zone that reached them would have bleached a hedge.
# 1.45 less the rule's own 0.035 is 0.065 clear of the top of one. The band that is left is the
# podium's bed mould, fillet and corona — the part of the south face that is actually in the sun
# all day rather than in the shadow of its own cornice.
let s_sun_podium  = box   -16.60  1.45 -13.12  16.60  1.78 -12.76
let s_sun_zone    = union { s_sun_cornice s_sun_ashlar s_sun_podium }
let s_sun_face    = max { s_sun_zone  add { constant -0.10  negate { s_bloom } } }
paint marble_hot where=s_sun_face below=0.035

# North: a dark film of algae on the wall face between the top of the overgrown zone (2.40) and the
# bottom of the string course (5.31). It is heaviest at the bottom, where the wall stays wet
# longest, so the fade runs the other way — one at 2.45 and nought at 5.30.
let s_shade_zone = box -16.12 2.45 7.45  16.12 5.30 7.66
let s_shade_fade = smoothstep { axis of=y } from=5.30 to=2.45
let s_shade_wet  = multiply { s_shade_fade s_bloom }
let s_shade_face = max { s_shade_zone  add { constant 0.08  negate { s_shade_wet } } }
paint verde where=s_shade_face below=0.02

# ================================================================================================
# 3 — THE RUN-OFF, over the top of the aspect
# ================================================================================================

# The grime. About a fifth of the band, in runs a third of a metre wide and two or three long.
let s_str_run = max { s_str_zone  add { constant 0.24  negate { s_str_wet } } }
paint tuff where=s_str_run below=0.035

# And the few near-black runnels inside them, where the same water has run down the same line for
# a hundred years. Same zone, same pattern, a higher threshold — so these are a subset of the grey
# ones by construction and cannot land anywhere the grey does not.
#
# 0.34 AND NOT 0.52, for the same reason the iron stain is 0.42 and not 0.58, and found the same
# way. At 0.52 this rule was asked 183,312 times and painted NOTHING, and the only number in any
# report that said so was a material histogram with `slate` at zero. `clipcheck` cannot see it:
# the rule fired, the components are unchanged, and 183,312 evaluations look like a rule doing its
# job. TWO OF THE SEVENTEEN RULES IN THIS FILE WERE WRITTEN THIS WAY AND BOTH WERE INVISIBLE.
let s_str_dark = max { s_str_zone  add { constant 0.34  negate { s_str_wet } } }
paint slate where=s_str_dark below=0.035

# THE SPLASH BAND. Rain hitting the terrace and the ground throws earth back up against whatever
# stands in it, and it never reaches higher than about two thirds of a metre. So: the foot of the
# wall above the terrace, and the podium's rusticated die above the ground. Warm and earthy rather
# than grey, because what is in it is soil and not soot.
let s_spl_wall_s = mirror { box  7.70 1.78 -7.66  16.12 2.45 -7.45 } axis=x
let s_spl_wall_n = box   -16.12 1.78  7.45  16.12 2.45  7.66
let s_spl_wall_e = mirror { box 15.95 1.78 -7.62  16.17 2.45  7.62 } axis=x
let s_spl_pod_s  = box   -16.50 0.45 -12.90  16.50 1.10 -12.76
let s_spl_pod_n  = box   -16.50 0.45   7.81  16.50 1.10   7.94
let s_spl_pod_e  = mirror { box 16.36 0.45 -12.90  16.49 1.10 7.94 } axis=x
let s_spl_zone   = union { s_spl_wall_s s_spl_wall_n s_spl_wall_e
                           s_spl_pod_s s_spl_pod_n s_spl_pod_e }
let s_splash     = max { s_spl_zone  add { constant 0.06  negate { s_bloom } } }
paint sandstone where=s_splash below=0.035

# ================================================================================================
# 4 — THE DRY SOFFITS: what rain never reaches stays pale
# ================================================================================================
#
# This is the half of weathering that everybody forgets, and it is the half that makes a
# photograph look like a photograph: the underside of a projection is CLEANER and LIGHTER than the
# face above it, because nothing has ever washed over it and nothing has ever stained it. Four of
# them, taken at their real heights:
#
#   1.50   the podium corona's soffit, from the fillet face (16.5375 / -12.9375 / 7.9875) out to
#          the corona face (16.65 / -13.05 / 8.10) — a 0.1125 m drip all the way round
#   5.40   the string course's soffit, from the neck (7.590) out to the band's face (7.680)
#  11.52   the main cornice's soffit, from the ovolo (-7.92) out to the corona face (-8.10)
#   9.90   the portico ceiling, five metres deep and coffered, which has never seen weather at all
#
# THE END WALLS ARE NOT THE SAME NUMBERS TURNED ROUND AND THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS GOT THREE OF
# THEM WRONG. A south zone is written in z as `-7.50 - projection`; the same band on the east front
# is `16.00 + projection`, and copying the z figures across gives 16.06..16.30 where the cornice
# soffit actually is 16.42..16.60 — a zone buried inside the corona's own mass, which fires, paints
# nothing anybody can see, and looks exactly like success. Every `_e` box below is written from the
# projection: the entablature stands 0.72 out (corona face 0.60, crown 0.72), the string course
# 0.18, the parapet 0.18, the podium corona 0.65.
#
# The cornice band stops at |z| 7.66 / |x| 16.40 on the inboard side ON PURPOSE: the dentils are
# 0.090 m blocks with 0.045 m gaps at y 11.22..11.40 and z -7.67..-7.84, and a zone that reached
# them would paint the one piece of thin matter up there. They are 0.12 m below the bottom of this
# band and outside it in z as well.
let s_sof_pod_s = box  -16.72 1.44 -13.12  16.72 1.52 -12.90
let s_sof_pod_n = box  -16.72 1.44   7.93  16.72 1.52   8.17
let s_sof_pod_e = mirror { box 16.48 1.44 -13.12  16.72 1.52 8.17 } axis=x
let s_sof_str_s = mirror { box  7.70 5.34 -7.72  16.24 5.44 -7.56 } axis=x
let s_sof_str_n = box  -16.24 5.34   7.56  16.24 5.44   7.72
let s_sof_str_e = mirror { box 16.06 5.34 -7.72  16.24 5.44 7.72 } axis=x
let s_sof_cor_s = mirror { box  7.70 11.46 -8.16  16.30 11.56 -7.90 } axis=x
let s_sof_cor_n = box  -16.30 11.46   7.90  16.30 11.56   8.16
let s_sof_cor_e = mirror { box 16.40 11.46 -8.16  16.66 11.56 8.16 } axis=x
let s_sof_port  = box   -7.66  9.86 -11.20   7.66  9.98  -7.40
let s_sof_zone  = union { s_sof_pod_s s_sof_pod_n s_sof_pod_e
                          s_sof_str_s s_sof_str_n s_sof_str_e
                          s_sof_cor_s s_sof_cor_n s_sof_cor_e
                          s_sof_port }
# Nearly all of it, but not all: -0.45 leaves about a fifth of each soffit unpainted, so the pale
# is a coat with holes in it and not a second material with a hard edge.
let s_soffit    = max { s_sof_zone  add { constant -0.45  negate { s_bloom } } }
paint plaster where=s_soffit below=0.035

# ================================================================================================
# 5 — THE DIRTY TOPS: every horizontal is dirtier than the face under it
# ================================================================================================
#
# The other half of the same argument. A ledge collects what falls out of the air and holds the
# water that carries it, so the top of a string course, a cornice, a balustrade rail and the
# terrace edge are the darkest stone on the elevation.
#
# The three terrace pieces take only the OUTER 0.33 m of the ledge and not the whole width of it,
# and that is a budget decision with a number behind it. A paint rule keyed on a pattern cannot be
# settled for a block, so a box that overlaps a settled-solid block makes the sampler walk every
# voxel of that block for this one rule; the terrace bands are the widest zones in the file and
# they lie flat across the largest solid mass in the building. At 0.66 m wide this rule cost
# 1,082,655 field evaluations at metre 8, which was a fifth of the whole build's paint before this
# section existed. The outer third is also where the dirt is: the inner edge against the wall is
# where the soil cove goes, in section 9, and it is matter rather than colour.
let s_top_str_s = mirror { box  7.70 5.79 -7.72  16.24 5.89 -7.44 } axis=x
let s_top_str_n = box  -16.24 5.79   7.44  16.24 5.89   7.72
let s_top_str_e = mirror { box 15.94 5.79 -7.72  16.24 5.89 7.72 } axis=x
let s_top_cor_s = mirror { box  7.70 11.84 -8.28  16.40 11.96 -7.66 } axis=x
let s_top_cor_n = box  -16.40 11.84   7.66  16.40 11.96   8.28
let s_top_cor_e = mirror { box 16.16 11.84 -8.28  16.78 11.96 8.28 } axis=x
# THE SIX BRONZE URNS STAND IN THE MIDDLE OF THE RAIL ZONE AND ARE CUT OUT OF IT. `parapet_peds`
# puts a pedestal at (+-16.00, +-7.50) and at (+-8.10, -7.50) and `parapet_urns` stands the order's
# urn on each, bedded to y = 12.95 — so `urn_plinth` occupies 12.95..13.135, straight through a
# band meant for the top of a marble handrail. Grime and lichen on a bronze urn is a material
# error, not a subtle one: it would read as stone. So a 0.60 m square is taken out round each of
# the six, which costs six small unweathered patches of rail and keeps every rule in this file off
# the only bronze up there.
let s_urn_keepout = mirror { union {
    box 15.70 12.80  7.20  16.30 13.20  7.80
    box 15.70 12.80 -7.80  16.30 13.20 -7.20
    box  7.80 12.80 -7.80   8.40 13.20 -7.20
} } axis=x
let s_top_rail_s = difference { mirror { box  7.70 12.94 -7.74  16.20 13.06 -7.44 } axis=x
                                s_urn_keepout }
let s_top_rail_n = difference { box  -16.20 12.94   7.44  16.20 13.06   7.74  s_urn_keepout }
let s_top_rail_e = difference { mirror { box 15.94 12.94 -7.74  16.20 13.06 7.74 } axis=x
                                s_urn_keepout }
let s_top_ter_s = box  -16.72 1.74 -13.11  16.72 1.86 -12.78
let s_top_ter_n = box  -16.72 1.74   7.83  16.72 1.86   8.16
let s_top_ter_e = mirror { box 16.39 1.74 -13.11  16.72 1.86 8.16 } axis=x
let s_top_zone  = union { s_top_str_s s_top_str_n s_top_str_e
                          s_top_cor_s s_top_cor_n s_top_cor_e
                          s_top_rail_s s_top_rail_n s_top_rail_e
                          s_top_ter_s s_top_ter_n s_top_ter_e }
let s_tops      = max { s_top_zone  add { constant -0.28  negate { s_bloom } } }
paint tuff where=s_tops below=0.035

# ================================================================================================
# 6 — BIOLOGY, WHERE THE WATER SITS AND ONLY THERE
# ================================================================================================
#
# MOSS IN THE BED JOINTS OF THE NORTH RUSTICATION, and this is the one rule in the file that is
# aimed at a specific piece of somebody else's geometry rather than at a face. `walls.clip` cuts
# horizontal channels 0.09 wide and 0.135 deep on a 0.60 course — `walls_hchan` is a
# `repeat { } y=0.60 ny=2` translated to 3.60, so the beds are at y = 2.40, 3.00, 3.60, 4.20 and
# 4.80. A zone built the same way lands in them exactly, and a channel on a north wall is where
# moss on a building actually is: a horizontal slot that holds water on the face that never dries.
#
# The zone reaches from z 7.36 to 7.47, so it is INSIDE the wall face at 7.50 — which is solid
# stone except where a channel has taken it away, and the channel floor at 7.365 is what is left.
let s_moss_bed  = translate { repeat { box -16.12 -0.055 7.36  16.12 0.055 7.47 } y=0.60 ny=2 }
                            0 3.60 0
let s_moss_face = max { s_moss_bed  add { constant -0.05  negate { s_moss_n } } }
paint hedge where=s_moss_face below=0.035

# PALE LICHEN ON THE TOPS THAT STAND IN THE OPEN. A crustose lichen wants light and rain and no
# feet, which on this building is the cornice and the balustrade rail and nothing else — not the
# terrace, which is walked, and not the string course, which is in the splash of the storey above.
# A different seed from the grime, so the patches are not the same patches paler.
let s_lich_zone = union { s_top_cor_s s_top_cor_n s_top_cor_e
                          s_top_rail_s s_top_rail_n s_top_rail_e }
let s_lichen    = max { s_lich_zone  add { constant 0.34  negate { s_moss_n } } }
paint travertine where=s_lichen below=0.035

# ================================================================================================
# 7 — THE BRONZE, WHICH AGES NOTHING LIKE THE STONE IT IS SET IN
# ================================================================================================
#
# `doors.clip` hangs two bronze leaves in the great door (x +-1.35, y 1.80..6.30) and one pair in
# each side doorway (x 4.50..6.30 and mirrored, y 1.80..4.95). They stand under the portico, so
# rain does not fall on them — but they are bronze in a damp shaded loggia, which is the classic
# case: green where water creeps and runs, brown-gold bright where a hand has been on them daily
# for a century.
#
# THE TWO DOORS ARE AT DIFFERENT DEPTHS AND THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS MISSED ONE OF THEM.
# The great door's leaves are BOWED — `doors_gd_slab` is the skin between two cylinders of radius
# 5.40 and 5.265 struck from (0, -2.25) — so their face runs from z = -7.65 on the centre line to
# z = -7.479 at the jamb. The side doors are FLAT: `doors_sd_slab` is a box at z -7.41..-7.32, with
# its bead offset 0.045 in front of that. A single z band cannot hold both, so there are two.
#
# Both zones stop at x = 1.34 / 4.56 / 6.24, which keeps them off the marble doorcases:
# `doors_gd_f1_jamb` starts at x = 1.35, the astragal plate at 1.5075, and the side doorcase runs
# 3.96 to 6.84 with a void of 4.50 to 6.30 cut out of it. Inside those voids there is no stone at
# all — `doors_gd_void` and `doors_sd_void` take the wall, the dado band and its cap away — so
# everything these two zones can reach is bronze.
let s_door_gd = box  -1.34 1.82 -7.70   1.34 6.25 -7.42
let s_door_sd = mirror { box 4.56 1.82 -7.47  6.24 4.90 -7.30 } axis=x
let s_door    = union { s_door_gd s_door_sd }

# Verdigris where the water creeps: the bottom of a leaf, and the vertical runs down it. The first
# is broad and low, the second is narrow and long and goes on top of it.
let s_verd_low = box -6.30 1.82 -7.75  6.30 3.30 -7.28
let s_verd_z   = intersection { s_door s_verd_low }
let s_verd     = max { s_verd_z  add { constant -0.12  negate { s_bloom } } }
paint copper where=s_verd below=0.035

let s_verd_run = max { s_door  add { constant 0.46  negate { s_streak } } }
paint malachite where=s_verd_run below=0.035

# And the hand. The ring handles hang 1.08 m above the floor, so at y = 2.88 on a threshold of
# 1.80, and a door that is pushed every day is polished bright in a band round them and dulls
# again above and below. This is the one coat in the file that makes a surface BRIGHTER, and it is
# the reason the verdigris reads as verdigris rather than as green paint: the two are 0.4 m apart
# on the same leaf.
let s_hand_z = intersection { s_door  box -6.30 2.62 -7.75  6.30 3.16 -7.28 }
let s_hand   = max { s_hand_z  add { constant -0.35  negate { s_grit } } }
paint ormolu where=s_hand below=0.035

# ================================================================================================
# 8 — WEAR FOLLOWS PEOPLE
# ================================================================================================
#
# THE WALKED PATH. Everybody who enters this building crosses the portico floor on the centre line
# and goes through the great door, and a granite floor walked for a century is darker and smoother
# down the middle than it is at the edges. `slate` is the contract's dark smooth stone —
# 70,72,78 at roughness 190 against granite's 126,124,120 at 225 — which is exactly the difference
# between a walked flag and one nobody stands on.
#
# The band is 4.8 m wide at the door and the pattern frays it, so it is a path and not a carpet.
# It runs BETWEEN the two inner columns, whose plinths stand at x 0.675..2.025 on z = -11.15, and
# so it takes the bottom 0.06 m of those two plinths with it. That is left in deliberately: a dark
# line where a column foot meets a floor people walk on is what a walked floor does, and the
# alternative — a path that stops short of the columns — is a carpet again.
let s_walk_z = box -2.40 1.72 -12.60  2.40 1.86 -7.30
let s_walk   = max { s_walk_z  add { constant -0.34  negate { s_wash } } }
paint slate where=s_walk below=0.02

# THE DUST ON THE SHELTERED WALL. Under a five metre soffit nothing is ever washed, so what
# collects on the portico's back wall is dry wind-blown dust rather than a stain — pale, even, and
# with no run in it at all, which is why this is the one rule keyed on the blotchy pattern and not
# on the stretched one. The three pieces are the piers between the three doorways and the wall
# above their heads; the doorways themselves are bronze and are painted above.
let s_dust_a = mirror { box 1.90 1.82 -7.60  4.30 6.85 -7.45 } axis=x
let s_dust_b = mirror { box 6.50 1.82 -7.60  7.60 6.85 -7.45 } axis=x
let s_dust_c = box -7.60 6.90 -7.60  7.60 9.85 -7.45
let s_dust_z = union { s_dust_a s_dust_b s_dust_c }
let s_dust   = max { s_dust_z  add { constant -0.20  negate { s_bloom } } }
paint gravel where=s_dust below=0.035

# THE IRON STAIN. A classical cornice is cramped to the wall behind it with iron, and iron in a
# limestone cornice rusts and bleeds down the face below it in narrow rust-coloured runs. Rare —
# the threshold is the highest in the file — and only on the corona and crown of the main
# entablature, which is where the cramps are.
#
# 0.42 AND NOT 0.58, AND THE FIRST NUMBER IS WHY `clipcheck` ALONE IS NOT ENOUGH. At 0.58 this
# rule was evaluated 68,110 times on the whole building at metre 8 and painted NOT ONE VOXEL:
# `s_streak` is a four-octave fbm blended with a finer one, and it does not reach 0.58 often enough
# to land on a band 0.28 m deep. It is not in clipcheck's `never fired` list either, because that
# list is rules whose test was never ASKED, and this one was asked seventy thousand times. The only
# thing that caught it was the material histogram: terracotta came out 516 before and 516 after.
let s_rust_s = mirror { box  7.70 11.48 -8.30  16.34 11.92 -8.02 } axis=x
let s_rust_n = box  -16.34 11.48   8.02  16.34 11.92   8.30
let s_rust_e = mirror { box 16.52 11.48 -8.30  16.80 11.92 8.30 } axis=x
let s_rust_z = union { s_rust_s s_rust_n s_rust_e }
let s_rust   = max { s_rust_z  add { constant 0.42  negate { s_streak } } }
paint terracotta where=s_rust below=0.035

# ================================================================================================
# 9 — THE MATTER: two pieces of weathering that are shape and not colour
# ================================================================================================
#
# At the contract's 32 voxels to the metre a voxel is 0.03125 m. A moss cushion on a ledge stands
# two or three of those proud of it and a fillet of soil against a plinth is five or six across —
# both are MATTER at this resolution and painting them would be a lie. Both are also ADDITIVE,
# which is what makes them possible from this file at all (see the note at the top).
#
# Both are displaced by a noise at 0.05 m, which is 1.6 voxels at metre 32 and therefore survives
# `usable_displacement`'s half-a-voxel floor — anything smaller is dropped at parse time with a
# warning, and the manifest's own 0.012 m of grain is dropped exactly that way today.

# THE SOIL FILLET in the angle where the north wall stands on the podium terrace. Where a wall
# meets a flat on a side nobody walks, a cove of soil and leaf litter builds up against it; it is
# one of the most reliable things in a photograph of a building and one of the first things
# missing from a render. Built as a box in the corner with a cylinder taken out of it, which makes
# a concave cove rather than a triangular ramp, and lapped into the wall and into the terrace so it
# can never float — see the note below for how deep and why that number is not 0.05.
#
# NOT AT THE FOOT OF THE PLINTH, which was the first place I put it and is already occupied:
# `site_hedge_skirt_n` is a clipped hedge at z 8.100..8.500 and y -0.090..0.225 running the whole
# north front, and `site_hedge_skirt_e` does the same at |x| 16.65..17.00. A fillet of soil down
# there would have been built inside a hedge and painted over it. The terrace ledge north of the
# wall is 0.60 m of open stone between the wall face at 7.50 and the podium's corona at 8.10, and
# there is nothing on it: `part_terrace` is the ROOF garden, twelve metres up.
#
# THE LAP IS 0.08 AND NOT 0.05, AND THE DISPLACEMENT IS WHY. Rule 4 of the brief asks for 0.05 of
# overlap so a join cannot open; a displacement of +-0.05 on top of that can take all of it back
# and leave the cove touching the wall along a line rather than a face. Built at 0.05 it put ONE
# extra floating voxel into the finished building at metre 8 — 804 components before, 805 after.
# So the box laps 0.08 behind the wall face (7.42 against 7.50) and 0.08 under the terrace top
# (1.72 against 1.80), which is 0.03 of overlap left at the worst the noise can do.
let s_fillet_mass = box -16.00 1.72 7.42   16.00 1.97 7.70
let s_fillet_cut  = cylinder 0 1.97 7.70  r=0.25 h=33 axis=x
let s_fillet_raw  = difference { s_fillet_mass s_fillet_cut }
let s_fillet      = displace { s_fillet_raw s_bloom } amount=0.05

# THE MOSS CUSHIONS on the north string course. The band's top at y 5.85 runs from z 7.455 to
# 7.635, an 0.18 m ledge on the one elevation that never dries, and moss on a ledge is a cushion
# with a thickness — not a green film. Lapped 0.05 down into the band (5.80 against a top at 5.85)
# and displaced by half again its own height, so it is a row of separate cushions rather than a
# ridge: where the noise is low the surface drops below the stone and there is simply no moss.
# Same 0.08 lap for the same reason: the box starts at 5.74, which is 0.11 below the band's top at
# 5.85 and well inside the ovolo underneath it, so the bottom of a cushion is in stone whatever the
# noise does. The gaps are still there — where the noise runs the other way the top drops below
# 5.85 and the cushion is simply buried in the stone it sits on — and that is what makes this a row
# of cushions rather than a green ridge running thirty-two metres.
let s_cushion_raw = box -16.18 5.74 7.48   16.18 5.90 7.62
let s_cushion     = displace { s_cushion_raw s_moss_n } amount=0.05

let part_surface = union { s_fillet s_cushion }

# Painted last of everything in the building, which is what being the last include buys.
#
# KEYED ON THE DISPLACED SHAPE AND NOT ON THE BOX IT WAS CUT FROM, which is the opposite of what
# BRIEF.md rule 5 asks for and is right here for the reason that rule exists. The rule says
# `below=0.02` because the manifest displaces the finished building AFTER a fragment has painted
# it, so a rule keyed on the undisplaced shape has to reach out to catch the voxels the grain made.
# These two shapes carry their own displacement INSIDE the node the rule names, so the node's own
# surface is where the matter is: `below=0.03` on `s_fillet` is exactly the cove and nothing else.
# Keyed on `s_fillet_raw` instead it would need `below=0.06` to cover the same voxels, and 0.06 of
# reach off a box that runs the whole north front is a continuous green band on the stone under it
# — a uniform layer, which is the thing this whole section exists not to be.
paint bark  where=s_fillet  below=0.03
paint hedge where=s_cushion below=0.03
